{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 3, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Tripoli a noble family that ruled over the County of Tripoli a Crusader state in the Levant. He was the son of Pons of Tripoli and Hodierna of Toron and was born around 1140. He succeeded his father in 1152 and was a key figure in the politics of the Levant during the 12th century.\n\n* **Early Life and Reign** * \nRaymond III was a minor when he inherited the County of Tripoli from his father and his mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age. He was married to Humberge of Toron in 1163 but she died soon after and he then married Eschiva of Bures in 1165. He was known for his military prowess and was involved in several battles against the Muslim forces in the region. \n\nHe was a key ally of King Amalric I of Jerusalem and supported him in his campaigns against the Fatimid Caliphate. Raymond III also played a significant role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and was involved in several conflicts with the nobility. \n\n* **Battle of Montgisard and Battle of Marj Ayyun** *\nIn 1177 Raymond III led a Crusader army in the Battle of Montgisard against the forces of Saladin and the battle was a significant victory for the Crusaders. However he was defeated at the Battle of Marj Ayyun in 1179 and was forced to surrender to Saladin. He was imprisoned and later released but was forced to swear an oath not to wage war against Saladin.\n\n* **Later Life and Death** *\nRaymond III died in 1187 and was succeeded by his son Bohemond IV of Antioch who was only a minor at the time. Raymond III's death occurred during the Third Crusade and the County of Tripoli was eventually conquered by Saladin in 1188. Raymond III's life is notable for his military campaigns and his involvement in the politics of the Levant during the 12th century.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.014390614